  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unseasonable warmth and rain chances through\nFriday.\n\nA cold front was set to cross the Appalachian crest as of early\nafternoon. South to southwesterly breezes combined with cloud\nbreaks were pushing temperatures well into the 70s (60s in the\nmountains). Spotty light shower activity was developing ahead of\nthe front.\n\nAs the front crosses the I-81 corridor and eventually the Blue\nRidge/Catoctin Mountains this evening, it will slow and briefly\nstall due to stubborn high pressure offshore. Lift will increase\nvia continued warm/moist low-level advection and convergence, as\nwell as forcing for ascent ahead of the mid/upper trough and jet\nstreak. This will likely result in a broken band of showers and\na few thunderstorms near and east of I-95 through much of the\nnight. PWATs will remain in the 1-1.5\" range near and east of\nI-95 with heavy downpours possible. Around 500-1000 J/kg of\nMUCAPE will likely develop along the front due to mid-level\ncooling and low-level moistening, but the low levels should be\nrather stable. Aloft, lapse rates are rather modest through much\nof the column (though a bit steeper in upper levels). So while\nsome lightning-producing updrafts of moderate intensity are\nplausible, the risk for severe weather (tornadoes, hail, or\ndamaging winds) is very low. The risk for flooding is also very\nlow due to recent dry conditions.\n\nThe front will slowly begin to shift southeastward and out of\nthe area on Friday as a wave of low pressure develops and lifts\nnorth-northeast away from the region. Rain chances linger over\nsouthern Maryland into early Friday evening, with a few\nsprinkles or showers possible further west (especially along and\nwest of the Allegheny Front in upslope) as the upper trough\nswings through. Temperatures may cool enough to result in a mix\nor change to spotty light snow late Friday night into Saturday\nmorning for elevations above 3500 feet in the Appalachians as\nconditions dry and cool elsewhere on northwest breezes. These\nbreezes will usher in seasonably cooler air for the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably cool weather returns this weekend. \n\nHigh pressure will build near and south of the region this\nweekend. Northwest breezes are likely to stay elevated for many\nlocations (higher elevations, northern and eastern piedmont, the\nmetros, and over/near larger waterways like the Chesapeake Bay\nand tidal Potomac River). Closer to the high in the central\nShenandoah Valley into the central Virginia piedmont, winds may\ndecouple Saturday night and especially Sunday night resulting in\nthe potential for near freezing temperatures. The growing season\nremains active for all areas east of the Blue Ridge/Catoctin\nMountains except for western Loudoun County in Virginia, and \nFrederick County in Maryland. It is possible that patchy frost\nmay develop late Saturday night into early Sunday morning across\nareas the decouple in central Virginia, but frost/near freezing\ntemperatures are more likely Sunday night into Monday morning.\nStill, with the center of the high pressure to the south, there\nmay be enough of a light wind to keep the I-95 corridor and\nnorthern-to-central Maryland to prevent frost/freeze in these \nlocations. Otherwise, outside of some lingering light upslope\nalong and west of the Allegheny Front Saturday, the weekend\nlooks to turn out dry.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dry conditions early next week.\n\nAfter a brief wet and humid period late this week, conditions \nstart to dry out again this weekend in the wake of a cold \nfront. Upper-level ridging settles in overhead by Monday with \ngenerally clear conditions anticipated, although winds could \nremain breezy as the front transitions offshore. As upper-level\nridging transitions to zonal flow in the following couple of \ndays, conditions should continue to remain benign, with \ndiminishing winds by mid week.\n\nAfternoon highs drop back into the lower 60s in the immediate \naftermath of the front, but gradually increase again through \nWednesday as high pressure lingers. Expect somewhat above \nseasonable temperatures to persist as this dry pattern\ncontinues, though there are hints of a pattern shift toward the\nend of next week.",
